//? Title: Freivalds verification: projections, exact guarantees and numerical limits
//? Subtitle: Actual binary challenges, the original seed experiment and the work behind three matrix-vector products
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given A, B and a claimed product C, can we check the claim without computing a
fresh dense matrix product? Freivalds compares A(Br) with Cr for binary challenge
vectors r. A two-by-two example makes the projection and its blind spot visible.
The original forty-by-forty integer experiment is preserved through a native
once-per-seed sweep. The exact-arithmetic theorem, the library's absolute
floating-point tolerance and deterministic pseudorandom experiments have
different premises: this report derives the theorem and exhibits both a small
wrong product that passes and an exactly correct product that rounding rejects.
Actual operation counts and owned traces make those distinctions inspectable.
)

\toc

> One binary challenge exposes a wrong entry

Let

$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\3&4\end{pmatrix},\quad
B=I,\quad C=\begin{pmatrix}2&2\\3&4\end{pmatrix}.
$$
\alttext(A is the two-by-two matrix with rows one two and three four. B is identity. C changes A's upper left entry from one to two.)

For r=(1,0), Br=(1,0), A(Br)=(1,3), and Cr=(2,3).
The first residual has magnitude one, so the native verifier rejects.
The diagram below is populated from its actual supplied-challenge frame.
Every round computes three products: Br, A(Br), and Cr.


```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/freivalds_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/freivalds.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::FreivaldsMatrix a{{1,2},{3,4}},b{{1,0},{0,1}},c{{2,2},{3,4}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe(a,b,c,{1,0});
  const auto& f=t.frames.at(0);
  std::print("Actual challenge {}; Br {}; A(Br) {}; Cr {}; tested absolute residuals {}; accepted {}.\n",f.rv,f.br,f.abr,f.cr,f.difference,t.accepted);
  std::print("\n| supplied r | numerical verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n");
  for(const auto& r:std::vector<std::vector<std::uint8_t>>{{0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{1,1}}){
    const auto q=dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe(a,b,c,r);
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",r,q.accepted?"accept":"reject");
  }
  const auto audit=dm::algorithms::freivalds_binary_audit(a,b,c);
  std::print("\nAll binary challenges: {}; accepted {}; rejected {}. Failed round {}; failed row {}; compared rows {}.\n",audit.vectors,audit.accepted,audit.rejected,t.failed_round,t.failed_row,t.work.compared_rows);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;
  node.width=3.2;node.height=1.1;node.font_size=20;
  d.node("r",{0,0},std::format("r = {}",f.rv),node)
   .node("br",{4,2},std::format("Br = {}",f.br),node)
   .node("abr",{8,2},std::format("A(Br) = {}",f.abr),node)
   .node("cr",{4,-2},std::format("Cr = {}",f.cr),node)
   .node("v",{8,-2},std::format("row {}: residual {}\nreject",t.failed_row,f.difference.at(0)),node);
  d.edge("r","br","B").edge("br","abr","A").edge("r","cr","C")
   .edge("abr","v","compare").edge("cr","v","");
  d.text({4,4},"One actual challenge; all three matvecs finish",26).save_svg("build/freivalds_tiny.svg");
  if(t.accepted||audit.accepted!=2||audit.rejected!=2)throw std::runtime_error("tiny exact control");
}
```
