// result_end
\caption(The exact IID reference is a mathematical comparison. The linear panel retains zero of4000 at rounds eleven and twelve; the logarithmic panel shows only positive observed fractions.)
\alttext(Two native plots compare the deterministic accepted fractions with two to the minus rounds. The linear plot includes two zeros; the log2 plot omits those zero fractions and applies no display floor.)

The counts are 1982,945,465,240,123,52,19,7,4,1,0,0. Zero observed accepts
means only that these 4000 seeds found the discrepancy within that prefix.
It does not imply zero error probability for a different sampling process.

The once-per-seed experiment performs 7838 rounds. The original independent
reruns perform 86,667 rounds and 416,001,600 multiply-accumulate terms; the new
sweep performs 37,622,400 such terms. These are executed kernel counts, excluding
validation, allocation, RNG implementation details and formatting. They describe
work avoided by prefix reuse, not a measured elapsed-time speedup.

> Numerical acceptance has different semantics

The double API rejects when an actually compared absolute residual exceeds
1e-6. It retains that policy for compatibility. It does not compute an exact
matrix product as a hidden precheck.

For A=B=[1] and C=[1+1e-7], both possible challenges pass the tolerance even
though the product is wrong. Rounding can also cause the opposite outcome.
Consider the exactly represented inputs

$$
A=(10^{16},1,-10^{16}),\quad
B=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&0\\1&1\end{pmatrix},\quad C=(1,0).
$$
\alttext(A has entries ten to the sixteen, one and negative ten to the sixteen. B has rows one one, one zero and one one. The exact product is the row one zero.)

Their exact integer product really is C. But for r=(1,0), the ordered double
sum for A(Br) loses the middle one and produces zero, while Cr is one.
The supplied-vector trace rejects. Therefore acceptance is a numerical residual
decision; rejection is also a numerical decision, with no general one-sided
exact-equality guarantee. Exhaustive binary audits below count that same
floating test and are not independent exact-arithmetic oracles.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/freivalds_numerical.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/freivalds.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::FreivaldsMatrix a{{1e16,1,-1e16}},b{{1,1},{1,0},{1,1}},c{{1,0}};
  const auto rounded=dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe(a,b,c,{1,0});
  std::print("Exact-product cancellation probe: A(Br) {}; Cr {}; absolute residual {}; accepted {}.\n",rounded.frames[0].abr,rounded.frames[0].cr,rounded.frames[0].difference,rounded.accepted);
  const auto good=dm::algorithms::freivalds_binary_audit({{1}},{{1}},{{1}});
  const auto tiny=dm::algorithms::freivalds_binary_audit({{1}},{{1}},{{1+1e-7}});
  const auto large=dm::algorithms::freivalds_binary_audit({{1}},{{1}},{{2}});
  const auto cancel=dm::algorithms::freivalds_binary_audit(a,b,c);
  const std::array<dm::algorithms::FreivaldsBinaryAudit,4> audits{good,tiny,large,cancel};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Exact small","Wrong by 1e-7","Wrong by 1","Exact cancellation"};
  std::vector<double> fractions;
  std::print("\n| case | vectors | accepted | rejected |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<audits.size();++i){const auto& q=audits[i];std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[i],q.vectors,q.accepted,q.rejected);fractions.push_back(double(q.accepted)/double(q.vectors));}
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(fractions,names);
  plot.title("Exhaustive binary challenges under absolute tolerance 1e-6").xlab("Candidate; exactness is stated in the label").ylab("Fraction numerically accepted").y_limits(0,1.1).size(1100,540).save_svg("build/freivalds_numerical.svg");
  if(!tiny.accepted||cancel.accepted!=2||cancel.rejected!=2||rounded.accepted)throw std::runtime_error("numerical policy controls");
}
```
