\image(figures/freivalds_numerical.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Small tolerance errors can always pass, and cancellation can reject a correct product. Each bar exhausts its own binary challenge space: two vectors for the scalar examples, four for cancellation.)
\alttext(Four bars show accepted fractions one, one, one half and one half for an exact scalar product, a scalar wrong by one e minus seven, a scalar wrong by one, and the exactly correct cancellation example.)

All entry values must now be finite. Every row of A, B and C is validated for
rectangular shape before dimension compatibility, zero-round shortcuts or quotas;
NaNs and infinities are rejected as invalid input. Actual nonfinite accumulators
or compared residuals throw an overflow exception. The implementation evaluates
only the requested projections, so a supplied zero challenge need not overflow
merely because an unused dense AB entry would be too large. These checks do not
remove finite rounding error or underflow.

> Count three matvecs before claiming a saving

For A of shape m by k, B of shape k by n and C of shape m by n, each performed
round uses

$$
kn+mk+mn
$$
\alttext(One performed round uses k times n plus m times k plus m times n multiply-accumulate terms.)

multiply-accumulate terms, plus challenge generation and row comparisons.
For square inputs this is 3n² per round. K successful rounds cost3Kn² such terms;
the schoolbook multiplication reference is n³. Fewer terms therefore requires
3K less than n. This comparison excludes validation, allocation and other costs;
it is not an elapsed-time crossover or the operation count of Strassen.

At the original n=40 and default K=20, a correct candidate performs96,000 terms,
which exceeds the 64,000 schoolbook reference. The original claim that verification
necessarily costs a fraction of multiplication at that setting is unsupported.
A wrong candidate may stop much earlier. Even a row-zero failure occurs only
after all three matvecs for its round have been computed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/freivalds_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/freivalds.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double>x,actual,reference;
  std::print("| n | performed rounds | actual verifier terms | schoolbook reference terms | verifier/reference |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const std::size_t n:std::array<std::size_t,7>{4,8,16,32,40,64,128}){
    dm::algorithms::FreivaldsMatrix identity(n,std::vector<double>(n));for(std::size_t i=0;i<n;++i)identity[i][i]=1;
    const auto q=dm::algorithms::freivalds_verify_work(identity,identity,identity,20);
    const auto measured=q.work.br_terms+q.work.abr_terms+q.work.cr_terms;
    const double schoolbook=double(n)*double(n)*double(n);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.4f} |\n",n,q.work.rounds_completed,measured,schoolbook,double(measured)/schoolbook);
    x.push_back(double(n));actual.push_back(std::log2(double(measured)));reference.push_back(std::log2(schoolbook));
    if(measured!=60*n*n||!q.accepted)throw std::runtime_error("three matvec count");
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(x,actual,"Actual 20 rounds",{220,38,38},2).points(x,actual,"",{220,38,38},4)
    .lines(x,reference,"Schoolbook n^3",{37,99,235},2)
    .legend({"Actual 20 rounds","Schoolbook n^3"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}})
    .title("Term counts, not elapsed timings or Strassen counts").xlab("Square dimension n").ylab("log2 multiply-accumulate terms").size(1050,520).save_svg("build/freivalds_work.svg");
}
```
