> Owned evidence and whole-request admission

Ordinary verification retains no history. A trace owns the three input matrices
and each actual round frame. The trace entry forecast is

$$
E_{\rm input}+T(1+n+k+3m),\qquad T=\max(K,0),
$$
\alttext(Trace admission is the sum of input row and cell entries plus the nonnegative requested rounds times one plus n plus k plus three m.)

for compatible dimensions; incompatible inputs have no round frames.
Input entries count all rows and scalar cells in A, B and C. A frame costs one
metadata record, n challenge entries, k entries in Br, and up to m entries each
in A(Br), Cr and the tested residual vector. These are entries, not bytes or peak
RAM. The default quota is one million entries; zero disables only that optional
quota. Count and container bounds still apply. Admission checks the conservative
whole requested history before input copies and RNG draws; it does not predict
an early mismatch or truncate a trace.

The tiny supplied challenge has an upper bound of 29 entries but actually retains 28,
because only its first residual is tested. A cap of 28 still rejects the request.
The exact cap 29 admits it. Binary enumeration has a separate vector-count cap.
Nonpositive rounds preserve legacy vacuous acceptance for compatible valid
inputs, so they provide no verification evidence. Even three empty matrices
perform the requested number of empty rounds with no random draws.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/freivalds_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/freivalds.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::FreivaldsMatrix a{{1,2},{3,4}},b{{1,0},{0,1}},c{{2,2},{3,4}};
  const auto forecast=dm::algorithms::freivalds_trace_entries(a,b,c,1);
  bool denied=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe(a,b,c,{1,0},28);}catch(const std::length_error&){denied=true;}
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe(a,b,c,{1,0},29);
  const auto& f=t.frames[0];
  const auto retained=18+1+f.rv.size()+f.br.size()+f.abr.size()+f.cr.size()+f.difference.size();
  a[0][0]=900;c.clear();const bool owned=t.a[0][0]==1&&t.c[0][0]==2;
  const auto empty=dm::algorithms::freivalds_trace({}, {}, {});
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::freivalds_verify_work({{1}},{{1}},{{2}},0);
  bool ragged=false,nan=false,overflow=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::freivalds_verify({{1,0},{0}},{{1,0},{0,1}},{{1,0},{0,1}},0);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){ragged=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::freivalds_verify({{NAN}},{{1}},{{1}},0);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){nan=true;}
  const double huge=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe({{huge}},{{2}},{{0}},{1});}catch(const std::overflow_error&){overflow=true;}
  const bool unused=dm::algorithms::freivalds_probe({{huge}},{{2}},{{0}},{0}).accepted;
  std::print("Tiny admission: upper {}; actual retained {}; cap 28 rejected {}; cap 29 admitted true; owned after caller edit {}.\n",forecast,retained,denied,owned);
  std::print("Empty default: accepted {}; actual frames {}; RNG words {}; forecast entries {}.\n",empty.accepted,empty.frames.size(),empty.work.random_words,dm::algorithms::freivalds_trace_entries({}, {}, {}));
  std::print("Wrong scalar with zero rounds: accepted {}; performed rounds {}; RNG words {}.\n",zero.accepted,zero.work.rounds_completed,zero.work.random_words);
  std::print("Domain checks: ragged rejected {}; NaN rejected {}; actual overflow rejected {}; unused large product with r0 accepted {}.\n",ragged,nan,overflow,unused);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(forecast),double(retained),28.},{"Whole upper bound","Actual retained","Rejected cap"});
  plot.title("A row 0 mismatch does not lower whole-request admission").xlab("Entry count for this one-round tiny trace").ylab("Retained-history entries; not bytes").y_limits(0,33).size(1050,520).save_svg("build/freivalds_controls.svg");
  if(!denied||!owned||!ragged||!nan||!overflow||!unused||forecast!=29||retained!=28)throw std::runtime_error("admission and validation");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Tiny admission: upper 29; actual retained 28; cap 28 rejected true; cap 29 admitted true; owned after caller edit true.
Empty default: accepted true; actual frames 20; RNG words 0; forecast entries 20.
Wrong scalar with zero rounds: accepted true; performed rounds 0; RNG words 0.
Domain checks: ragged rejected true; NaN rejected true; actual overflow rejected true; unused large product with r0 accepted true.
\image(figures/freivalds_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Admission uses the whole-request upper bound 29 even though the actual rejecting frame retains 28 entries. The cap is a retained-history entry quota and does not measure process memory.)
\alttext(Three bars show the whole trace upper bound twenty-nine, actual retained twenty-eight and rejected cap twenty-eight.)

The seeded kernel, supplied probe, binary audit and seed sweep share the same
projection implementation and actual work counters. Traces retain the values
that were computed; no replay fills in untested residuals. Ordinary calls keep
the legacy argument order, default twenty rounds and default seed, including
deterministic challenge consumption and early exits.

An accepted product is evidence only under the chosen arithmetic, tolerance,
challenge process and positive round count. For the bounded integer fixture,
the exact projection interpretation is useful and fully checkable. For general
double inputs, inspect scale and rounding behavior before interpreting that
numerical verdict as a statement about exact equality.
